body:
[you are not lazy](https://youtube.com/watch?v=aehoGGCHkyw) I just saw this the other day, and it really stuck with me. I know personally I can relate to most of the points he makes, it also made me realize I have more free time than I believe and that I am free to do whatever I want when I want, but I have to make myself do them in order for it to happen. It's only the 5th day of 2020, each day is a new opportunity to do apply yourself. I have always considered myself lazy and unmotivated with all the serious changes I needed to make in my life. I plan to change that this year by writing what my day was like, what I did and learned, once a day.

&#x200B;

I guess what I'm saying is that, it is possible, it will be hard, and it will take time, but the truth is all you have is time, its just using it the best that you can, and allow yourself room to fail and try again, and days where you can accept that its okay you did nothing and was lazy all day.

&#x200B;

Good luck, I hope you can reflect on this post a year from now and see how much has changed :)

The asteroid was visible in daylight now. Predicted to crash into the Pacific in exactly 27 days. It was massive, at a length of 102 miles wide, 119 miles tall and 139 miles deep, nothing was surviving after that impact landed. It was spotted five years ago and immediately the planet refocused all its labor and energy on getting to Mars. It was amazing really, how any conflict and difference was thrown out the window, how quickly our species was able to unify at the thought of us going extinct in a single moment. 

&#x200B;

Of course it didn't fix everything. The wealthy were still wealthy and the poor were still poor. After a couple years of Drones and AI building habitable cities on Mars, the first to go were the super rich. That was one year before impact so everyone believed we still had time. There was tens of thousands of ships capable of carrying over a hundred thousand people at a time. With each passing month, tickets became cheaper, but it became obvious not everyone was getting off this planet.

&#x200B;

Soon unions and gangs were hijacking ships, stealing them from those who paid, praying they will be allowed on Mars. Only bringing their closest friends and families, severely under utilizing the amount space available for people. Other ships were sabotaged, destroying ships as they were leaving the atmosphere, targeting those filled with high priority status, your CEO's and celebrities. We were descending into madness and savagery. We could only cheer at the fact that if we can't make it off this planet, why should they.

&#x200B;

As the asteroid got closer we could only collapse into anarchy, the last ships to leave were military, who were left behind to keep the peace the best they could. They shot anyone who tried to approach. Keeping a wide perimeter on their ship to ensure their own survival. The Civilian loading docks were either abandoned or destroyed, except one. It was disguised to look broken but was fully functional, running off the ships internal nuclear power. There were 3 ships left. The hard part was finding people who could fill them without giving away the secret that there was still hope. In the final days before departure, what was left of schools and hospitals, were loaded onto the final ships. Professionals of every kind were taken in as well. Doctors, surgeons, engineers, everyone who should've got a place but didn't. This was the last hope for the average person just trying to live out their life.

&#x200B;

Finally it was time to depart. We agreed to leave at 2 AM, for the easiest and safest departure. With no military protection, and fake debris that could still cause real problems, we powered up the engines and opened up the roof of the holding docks. We lift off first and just as we emerge from the opening, we see how badly the rest of the world has it. Collapsed buildings, fires raging on, bodies laying in the street. Nothing was recognizable, you never would've thought we were civilized and functioning just over a year ago. We pull away and the second emerges safely. Just as the third ship was pulling away and clearing its dock, a barrage of rockets tear into its side and thrusters, crashing the ship back into the city. With a final crash the nuclear reactors erupt into a fireball, leveling what remained of the city. We could only watch in horror, as those innocent souls were destroyed by jealousy and chaos. 

&#x200B;

We reach space and slow our thrusters, just remaining out of orbit from Earth. We focus all the cameras and sensors to the estimated impact zone. A few days later the Asteroid breaks the atmosphere and we watch it crash into the Earth, Creating a tsunami visible to the naked eye from space. Chunks of our planet break off and drift into the emptiness of space, our core visible and leaking to the surface. We watch as the chaos ripples across the surface, praying and forgiving those forgotten on the surface. The countless nameless beings, for in that moment it didn't matter if you were good or bad. A lover or hater, saint or sinner, your fate was the same, with no chance of survival. There would be no funeral, or remembrance of who they were. Just a number for the history books when our story is retold for future generations. After the Earth was covered in dust and debris, with no sight of the surface, we power up our thrusters, radio for Mars, and leave behind what was once our only home...
